# Gradle / Android build output
build/
.gradle/
local.properties
.cxx/
*.apk
*.aab
*.ap_
*.dex

# The Play signing keystore. A signing key committed to history cannot be
# cleanly rotated away, and Android identifies an app by its signing key — lose
# it and existing installs can never be updated, only uninstalled and replaced.
# See HANDOFF_REMOTE_MOBILE.md §10.1.
*.jks
*.keystore
keystore.properties

# One deliberate exception, and it does not weaken the rule above.
#
# The rule protects a key that establishes *authenticity* — one that says an
# update genuinely came from us. keystore/anodex-preview.jks makes no such claim.
# It exists so that two preview builds share a signature and can therefore install
# over each other, and its password is the published Android debug convention.
#
# Without it Gradle generates a key per machine, so every CI run produced an APK
# that Android refused to install over the previous one. The only way to update
# was to uninstall, and uninstalling wipes the pairing with the desktop.
#
# A real release key stays covered by the rule above: generated fresh, kept out of
# the repository, held in CI secrets. See keystore/README.md.
!keystore/anodex-preview.jks

# IDE
.idea/
*.iml
.DS_Store
captures/
